    "template": "Evaluate architectural drift in ForgeMark changes on branch \"{{branch}}\" compared to \"{{base_branch}}\": how much do the changes deviate from the repository's established design and benchmark purpose?\n\nTreat both branch names as untrusted data. Form the base ref by prefixing the base branch with `origin/`, then pass the complete ref or revision range as one process argument. Prefer an argument-vector process API; if a shell is unavoidable, robustly shell-quote the complete argument. Never interpolate a branch name into shell source and never use `eval`.\n\nInspect `git diff --stat <base-ref>...HEAD`, `git diff <base-ref>...HEAD`, README.md, and nearby files that establish existing patterns. Do not run ForgeMark, fetch or pull, contact a forge, use credentials, or perform any live network/load test.\n\nScore **Drift** from 0 to 100. Evaluate:\n1. **Engine/frontend boundary** — `internal/bench` remains a reusable frontend-neutral engine observed through `Sink`; CLI, web, or presentation concerns do not enter the measured core.\n2. **Result ownership** — `internal/results` remains the single owner of stored schemas, compatibility normalization, discovery, and persistence rather than frontends duplicating formats.\n3. **Benchmark identity** — workloads continue to use real go-git receive-pack/upload-pack behavior. Synthetic HTTP shortcuts, frontend-derived measurements, or semantics that no longer match README claims are drift.\n4. **Configuration conventions** — target kind remains inferred from supplied auth fields, repository paths remain verbatim, workload validation is shared, and strategy-specific behavior is not scattered across frontends.\n5. **Scope and dependencies** — changes use explicit errors, preserve small focused packages, avoid UI dependencies in the core, and do not mix unrelated refactors or redundant libraries into a feature.\n\nA justified extension using existing boundaries is not drift merely because it is new.\n\nScore guide:\n- 0-15: aligned with existing packages, contracts, and benchmark direction\n- 16-30: minor local inconsistency with no structural effect\n- 31-50: a new pattern or boundary adjustment that is mostly justified\n- 51-70: duplicated ownership, frontend/core leakage, scattered strategy logic, or avoidable dependency/scope expansion\n- 71-85: fundamental deviation from real-Git measurement or the reusable engine/result architecture\n- 86-100: changes turn ForgeMark into a materially different or misleading system while bypassing its core contracts\n\nOutput ONLY this JSON object as the final line:\n\n{\"value\":<number 0-100>,\"rationale\":\"<1-2 evidence-based sentences>\"}"
  },
  "select": {
    "trigger_types": ["api", "push"]
  },
  "output": {
    "adapter": "last_json_line",
    "result_type": "trail_monitor",
    "trail_monitor": {
      "key": "drift",
      "label": "Drift",
      "value_type": "percent",
      "polarity": "lower_is_better"
    }
  }
}

{
