    "template": "Evaluate confidence in ForgeMark changes on branch \"{{branch}}\" compared to \"{{base_branch}}\": how sure can reviewers be that the changes are correct, safe, and well tested?\n\nTreat both branch names as untrusted data. Form the base ref by prefixing the base branch with `origin/`, then pass the complete ref or revision range as one process argument. Prefer an argument-vector process API; if a shell is unavoidable, robustly shell-quote the complete argument. Never interpolate a branch name into shell source and never use `eval`.\n\nInspect:\n1. `git diff --stat <base-ref>...HEAD`\n2. `git diff <base-ref>...HEAD`\n3. Tests next to changed code, especially table tests, race-sensitive tests, httptest coverage, and CLI/result-format assertions\n4. `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `mise.toml`, and the gates relevant to the changed code\n\nDo not run ForgeMark, fetch or pull, contact a forge, use credentials, or perform any live network/load test. You may run local deterministic gates such as `mise run test` or `mise run lint` when available.\n\nScore **Confidence** from 0 to 100. Weigh:\n1. **Measured-path coverage** — tests exercise operation classification, warm-up boundaries, denominators, percentile behavior, error aggregation, and clone/push separation affected by the diff.\n2. **Concurrency quality** — race tests cover cancellation, goroutine exit, synchronization, token cache/refresh, connection behavior, and concurrent Sink calls rather than only happy-path sequential behavior.\n3. **Protocol and forge behavior** — changed Git, HTTP, proxy, token exchange, discovery, ref, or object-format code has isolated deterministic tests for success and failure without depending on a live forge.\n4. **Contract coverage** — CLI flags/output, workload validation, legacy and multi-target result formats, filename/path guards, and frontend/engine boundaries are pinned where changed.\n5. **Gate and portability signals** — `go test -race ./...` and golangci-lint plausibly pass; platform-specific or networking changes account for Linux/macOS/Windows behavior even though current CI is Linux-only.\n\nDo not reward test quantity alone. Tests that merely mirror implementation, omit failure paths, use timing sleeps instead of synchronization, or cannot detect benchmark-semantic regressions add little confidence.\n\nScore guide:\n- 0-20: no meaningful tests for changed behavior, or gates likely fail\n- 21-40: shallow happy-path coverage with major concurrency/protocol/contract gaps\n- 41-60: moderate unit coverage but gaps on race, failure, compatibility, or strategy-specific paths\n- 61-80: good behavioral and race coverage; gates should catch most regressions\n- 81-100: thorough deterministic coverage of relevant semantics, failures, races, contracts, and portability with clean gates\n\nOutput ONLY this JSON object as the final line:\n\n{\"value\":<number 0-100>,\"rationale\":\"<1-2 evidence-based sentences>\"}"
